record legeos.decision.window-groups-are-explicit-and-compositor-owned/1 : decision {
    title "Window groups are explicit, owned by lege-window, stacked as one, placed beside on grouping, outlined as one, and one entry on the bar; Super+Tab across groups and Alt+Tab across every window"
    state proposed
    scope [
        path "compositor/lege-comp/**",
        path "crates/lege-field/**",
        path "crates/lege-window/**",
        path "interfaces/wayland/**",
        path "shell/lege-shell/**"
    ]
    topic window-groups
    decision """
        A window group is an explicit, user-made set of two or more application windows that behave as one unit of work. Seven rules: (1) groups are explicit — made by dropping one window's title band on another's band, or by the Super+G chord; never inferred from proximity, application or time (docs/homefield.md's proximity rule). (2) The compositor owns groups through WindowService: membership lives in lege-window, the shell learns it over lege_shell_v1 (toplevel v3 `group` event, manager v5), so Alt+Tab and Super+Tab work with the shell dead. (3) A group is one thing to stack: activating any member raises every member contiguously, relative order kept, the activated one on top; geometry never changes. (4) Ungrouped windows are the loose set: Super+Tab treats it as one group and does nothing when there are no groups at all. **Alt+Tab is never scoped to a group**: it walks every unshelved client window, most-recently-activated first, whether or not any of them are grouped. Scoping it to the focused window's group (the original rule, withdrawn 2026-09-08) made a group a place Alt+Tab could not leave, and the user reported being unable to reach a window buried under others. Both switchers walk a list frozen at the first press and released with the modifier (lege_window::SwitchRing), because every step activates, activation raises, and the list is the stacking order — a list rebuilt per press can only swap the top two windows. (5) A group has at least two members; it dissolves when the second-to-last leaves or closes, and its bar entry goes with it. (6) Grouping places, it never merges: the dropped or chorded window is set down beside the target on the nearest free side, edge to edge; nothing is stacked, tabbed or folded into one frame (no iOS-style folder); only the band is a drop target, so ordinary overlapping placement is untouched; a band drag moves the whole group and Shift+drag moves one member. (7) A group is visibly one thing: a two-pixel outline in the group's accent around the union of the members' rectangles, the same colour as a stripe in each member's band and as its bar entry; the accent is assigned from a short palette by group id in lege-window, and the outline is per-window compositor chrome in stacking order. Chords: Alt+Tab / Alt+Shift+Tab across every window; Super+Tab / Super+Shift+Tab across groups by each group's last-active member; Super+G group with the previously focused window; Super+Shift+G ungroup. There is no within-group chord, no Ctrl+Tab binding and no setting for either. A group appears once on the bar's taskbar, keyed `group:<id>` — never on Home.
        """
    context """
        The user asked on 2026-09-03 for grouped windows that can be dragged together on Home as one window group, with Alt+Tab switching inside a group and Ctrl+Tab between groups. On reading the plan the user chose Super+Tab outright (Ctrl+Tab belongs to tabbed applications, and a compositor chord would take it from them), and specified that dropping one window on another must place it beside the other rather than combine them, with a coloured outline around the whole group as the indicator. lege-window already has a stored-nothing spatial step (Super+Left/Right via contiguous_window) and a shelf; lege-field already has container tiles (Project) and rigid group moves (MoveMany).

        Revised 2026-09-08. The Home half of this decision is withdrawn. A hands-on run of the installed session found Home crowded with things that are not the user's own, and docs/garden/garden.md draws the line: Home deals with your stuff, the bar deals with the machine. A running window — and so a group of them — is work in progress, not something you keep, so it lives on the bar's taskbar and nowhere else. What is withdrawn is only the placement: groups stay explicit, compositor-owned, placed beside, outlined as one, and Alt+Tab/Super+Tab are unchanged.
        """
    consequences """
        Plan in docs/planning/window-groups-and-application-integration.md §2–3 and @legeos.work.window-groups (WG1 model, WG2 compositor, WG3 protocol, WG4 Home tile, WG5 hands-on). The existing Super+Left/Right spatial step keeps its stored-nothing semantics and is renamed window.step-right/left in the action manifest so 'group' means one thing. Applications keep Ctrl+Tab. The group accent palette becomes per-theme when the two themes land (@legeos.decision.appearance-is-a-working-field-with-two-themes).

        From the 2026-09-08 revision: WG4's Home tile is superseded by the bar entry (@legeos.work.bottom-bar-taskbar, whose `taskbar::Entry::key` is already `group:<id>`). `field_model::merge_window_groups` and `merge_sessions` are deleted; the merge's existing sweep removes the `WindowGroup` and `ParkedSession` tiles a saved field still carries, as ordinary invertible `Remove` ops. Grouping by dropping one session tile on another on Home goes with the tiles; the band drop and Super+G remain, so rule (1) keeps two routes and `window.group`'s pointer route stays the compositor's own `POINTER_GESTURES` declaration. `FieldScreen` keeps its `WindowGroup`/`ParkedSession` drawing and interaction arms for now, marked `// ponytail:`, because the taskbar's hover row is their successor consumer.
        """
    implements [ @legeos.decision.home-is-momentary-and-placeable-objects-are-containers ]
}
